<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Population Projection_q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Using the following data about the female population, prepare a projection of the number of females in the year \(2001\).</p>
<p>
<table border="solid">
  <tr>
    <th width="15%", style="text-align:center">Age</th>
    <th width="15%", style="text-align:center">\(1961\)</th>
    <th width="15%", style="text-align:center">\(1981\)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(0-19\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_1a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_1b@}\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(20-39\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_2a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_2b@}\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(40+\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_3a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@ageset_3b@}\)</td>
  </tr>
</table>
</p>
<br>
<p>Number of females born between \(1961-1981\) was \({@births@}\), all born to women between \(20-39\).</p>
<hr>
<p>Note: Leave your answer correct to \(3\) dp</p>
<p>Calculate the survivorship ratios; you may assume that \(P_2=P_3\).</p>
<p>\(P_{1} = \) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>\(P_{2} = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>Find the Probability of a baby born surviving to time \(t+20\) which is denoted as \(P_{b} =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p>Find the fertility rate \(f_j^*\) for ages \(20-39   =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p></p>
<p>The projected population in 2001 will be</p>
<p>Note: Round your answer to the nearest integer</p>
<p>
<table border="solid">
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(0-19\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">[[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]]</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(20-39\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">[[input:ans6]] [[validation:ans6]][[feedback:prt6]]</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(40+\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center"> [[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]][[feedback:prt7]]</td>
  
  </tr>
</table>
</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="plain_text">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>First, We arrange a schematic diagram for the population distribution as follows;</p>
<p>This is age interval of 20 years.</p>
<p>\(f_j^*\) the fertility rate at ages groups</p>
<p>\(V(1,t)=\) population aged \(0-19\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(2,t)=\) population aged \(20-39\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p>\(V(3,t)=\) population aged \(40+\) years last birth day at time \(t\)</p>
<p></p>
<p>\(P_b=\) probability of a baby born surviving to time \(t+20\)</p>
<p>\(P_i=\) the probability of surviving from one age group to the next</p>
<hr>

<p>First, we arrange a schematic diagram for the population distribution. For \(t=1961\), we have:
<p>

<table border="solid" style="width: 100%;">
  <tr>
   <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">Age</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">\(1961\)</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">\(1981\)</th>
    <th width="25%" style="text-align:center">\(2001\)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
  <td style="text-align:center">\(0-19 \)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(1,t)={@ageset_1a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center"> \(V(1,t+20) ={@ageset_1b@}\)</td>
   <td style="text-align:center">\(V(1,t+40)\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
  <td style="text-align:center">\(20-39 \)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t) ={@ageset_2a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t+20) ={@ageset_2b@}\) </td>
  <td style="text-align:center">\(V(2,t+40)\) </td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td style="text-align:center">\(40+ \)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(V(3,t)= {@ageset_3a@}\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">  \(V(3+20) ={@ageset_3b@}\)</td>
   <td style="text-align:center">\(V(3,t+40)\)</td>

  </tr>
 
</table>
</p>
<hr>

<p>We start by calculating the survivorship ratios.</p>

<p>After \(20\) years, the surviving members of age group \(V(1,t)\) move to age group \(V(2,t+20)\). In mathematical terms: \(V(2,t+20) = V(1,t) P_1\). We can use this to compute the survivorship ratios:</p>
<p>\(P_1=\displaystyle \frac{V(2,t+20)}{V(1,t)}= \displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_2b@}}{{@ageset_1a@}}={@p1@}\)</p>

<p>For \(i=2\), i.e. the last age group, we have \(V(3,t+20) = V(2,t) P_2 + V(3,t) P_3\). We assume that \(P_2=P_3\), to get:</p>
<p>\(P_2=\displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_3b@}}{{@ageset_2a@}+{@ageset_3a@}}\)</p>
<p>\(P_2=P_3={@p2@}\)</p>

<p>Average number of women in the fertility group \(20-39\); </p>

<p>Computing the fertility rates:</p>
<p>\(f_2^*=\displaystyle \frac{\text{Total births}}{20 \cdot w_2}\)</p>
<p>Where, \(w_2=\displaystyle \frac{V(2,t)+V(2,t+20)}{2}=\displaystyle \frac{V(2,t)+V(1,t)P_1}{2}\)</p>
<p>so, \(f_2^*=\displaystyle \frac{{@births@}}{20 \left ( \displaystyle\frac{{@ageset_2a@}+{@ageset_1a@} \cdot {@p1@}}{2} \right )}={@fert_rate@}\)</p>
<p>Within the \(20\) year period \(1961-1981\), we have \({@births@}\) live births. Of those, \(V(1,t+20)={@ageset_1b@}\) survive until \(1981\), thus we get \(P_b = \displaystyle \frac{{@ageset_1b@}}{{@births@}} = {@pb@}\)</p>

<p>No. of surviving births \(=\displaystyle \frac{V(2,t)+V(2,t+20)}{2} \times 20 \cdot f_2^*  \cdot P_b\)</p>
<p>\(\quad \quad \quad \quad \quad=V(2,t)\cdot 10 \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b+  V(1,t)\cdot P_1 \cdot 10 \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b\) </p>
<p>Letting </p>
<p>\(f_1=P_1 \cdot 10 \cdot f_2^* \cdot P_b \) </p>
<p>\(f_2=10 \cdot f_2^*\cdot P_b \)</p>
<p>the above expression becomes \(=V(1,t)f_1+V(2,t)f_2\)</p>

<p>Hence we get our leslie matrix as follows</p>
<p>\(\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,t+20)\\ 
V(2,t+20)\\ 
V(3,t+20)
\end{bmatrix}=
\begin{bmatrix}
f_1 & f_2 & 0\\ 
P_1 & 0 & 0\\ 
0 &P_2  &P_3 
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,t)\\ 
V(2,t)\\ 
V(3,t)
\end{bmatrix}\)</p>
<hr>

<p>By evaluating this computation for \(t=1981\)</p>
<p>\(\begin{bmatrix}
V(1,2001)\\ 
V(2,2001)\\ 
V(3,2001)
\end{bmatrix}=
\begin{bmatrix}
f_1 & f_2 & 0\\ 
P_1 & 0 & 0\\ 
0 &P_2  &P_3 
\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
{@ageset_1b@}\\ 
{@ageset_2b@}\\ 
{@ageset_3b@}
\end{bmatrix}\)</p>

<hr>
<p>we get the following projection:</p>
<p>
<table border="solid">
<tr>
    <th width="15%", style="text-align:center">Age</th>
<th width="15%", style="text-align:center">\(2001\)</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(0-19\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@projection_1@}\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(20-39\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@projection_2@}\)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="text-align:center">\(40+\)</td>
    <td style="text-align:center">\({@projection_3@}\)</td>
  
  </tr>
</table>
</p>]]></text>
